# pg_heaptrack

Enable [heaptrack](https://github.com/KDE/heaptrack) heap profiling for a
running PostgreSQL backend, on demand, from SQL.

## Why this exists

A server built with `-DUSE_HEAPTRACK` (see `src/include/pg_config_manual.h`)
carries weak references to heaptrack's reporting API.  They are NULL — and the
instrumentation inert — unless heaptrack's tracker is loaded into the process.

The normal way to load it, the `heaptrack` wrapper, `LD_PRELOAD`s the tracker
into the postmaster; but heaptrack disables itself in forked children to avoid
two processes writing one data file, so every backend runs unprofiled.
Attaching with `heaptrack -p <pid>` records only block-level (malloc) traffic,
because the backend's weak `heaptrack_*` symbols were already bound to NULL at
startup.

`pg_heaptrack` loads heaptrack from *inside* an already-forked backend, where
the fork guard never tripped, and rewrites the backend's own GOT so the
instrumentation comes alive — including, optionally, the chunk-level
(palloc/pfree) layer.

## Requirements

- A server built with `-DUSE_HEAPTRACK` (needed only for chunk-level tracking;
  block-level works on any build).
- heaptrack installed, with `libheaptrack_inject.so` reachable by the dynamic
  loader (it is in the system library path per the deployment here).
- Linux or FreeBSD.  Loading the module on other platforms raises an error.

## Build and install

```sh
cd contrib/pg_heaptrack
make
make install
```

## Usage

Profile a single session:

```sql
LOAD 'pg_heaptrack';        -- recording starts immediately
-- run the queries you want to profile
SELECT pg_heaptrack_stop(); -- optional: flush now instead of at backend exit
```

Profile every backend (data file per PID):

```
session_preload_libraries = 'pg_heaptrack'
```

Do **not** use `shared_preload_libraries`: that loads the tracker into the
postmaster and re-creates the forked-child problem.  The module refuses it.

### Chunk-level tracking

By default only block-level (malloc) allocations are recorded.  To also
attribute individual `palloc`/`pfree` traffic — which requires a
`-DUSE_HEAPTRACK` server — set, before loading:

```sql
SET pg_heaptrack.track_chunks = on;
LOAD 'pg_heaptrack';
```

A build with `--enable-cassert` (hence `MEMORY_CONTEXT_CHECKING`) additionally
lets the context-reset reporting be exact rather than over-reporting freed
chunks.

## Configuration

| GUC | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pg_heaptrack.inject_library` | `libheaptrack_inject.so` | Path or soname of heaptrack's injection library. |
| `pg_heaptrack.output_prefix` | `/tmp/heaptrack.postgres` | Data-file prefix; the backend PID is appended. |
| `pg_heaptrack.track_chunks` | `off` | Also record palloc/pfree (needs `-DUSE_HEAPTRACK`). |

All three are `SUSET` (superuser-only).

## Analysing the result

Each backend writes one file, e.g. `/tmp/heaptrack.postgres.12345`:

```sh
heaptrack_gui   /tmp/heaptrack.postgres.12345
heaptrack_print /tmp/heaptrack.postgres.12345 | less
```

When chunk tracking is on, the profile contains two layers: block-level records
on `malloc`/`AllocSetAllocFromNewBlock` stacks, and chunk-level records on
`palloc`/`MemoryContextAlloc` stacks with the real call site above them.
Headline totals count both layers, so filter by call site to read one at a
time.

## Limitations

- Allocations made before `LOAD` are unknown to heaptrack; frees of them are
  ignored, and "leaked" means "allocated since LOAD and not yet freed".
- `palloc_aligned()` chunks are reported under their aligned pointer and show
  as leaked after a context reset (a `-DUSE_HEAPTRACK` limitation, not specific
  to this module).
- Recording carries heaptrack's usual cost: a global lock and a stack unwind
  per allocation, with palloc traffic far exceeding malloc traffic.  Profile
  focused workloads, not full benchmark runs.
